Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an attractive class of porous materials characterized by large surface areas and programmable structures, which have applications in a variety of setting s.1-12 MOFs are threedimensional networks in which metal ions or clusters are connected to multidentate ligands. Unlike nanoporous zeolites, several MOFs are characterized by flexible, periodic frameworks that can undergo reversible structural deformations, known as the “breathing” effect, upon the application of external stimuli (e.g., guest adsorption, temperature and pressure changes, light irradiation, and exposure to electric fields).13-21 Furthermore, the breathing in MOFs can be modulated by chemical functionalization of the framework.22 The ability to engineer the breathing behavior of MOFs at the molecular level thus holds promise for the development of multifunctional materials for applications as molecular switches, memory devices, chemical sensors, gas storage materials, and drug delivery systems.
MIL-53(M), with $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{l}$ , Sc, Cr, Fe, and Ga, is a prototypical family of flexible MOFs, exhibiting a large, yet reversible, structural transition from large pore (LP) to narrow pore (NP) configurations upon hydration. These transitions are accompanied by an anisotropic contraction of the unit cell volume up to $32\%$ .13 Besides water, MIL-53(M) materials also breathe upon adsorption of other molecular guests, including $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and aromatic compounds.23-25
Breathing structural transitions in MOFs induced by guest species are signaled by characteristic (multi)step profiles and large hysteresis in the corresponding adsorption isotherms.19 Adsorption measurements are usually supported by analysis of powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns, a bulk-average technique, combined with computer simulation s.16,26 However, direct observations of breathing in MOF nanocrystals are scarce, with no in situ single-particle studies of breathing behavior at the lattice level.16,26,27 In this study, we used in situ environmental transmission electron microscopy (ETEM) in combination with molecular dynamics (MD) simulations to directly monitor and discern the breathing behavior at the lattice-level of single MIL-53(Cr) nanocrystals during water adsorption/desorption, controlled through temperature ramping.
MIL-53(Cr) nanocrystal powders with sub-micron particle diameters, were synthesized according to standard literature conditions using anhydrous organic solvents. After washing the sample, the dried powder was characterized in bulk by PXRD (Figure S1) to verify the expected MIL-53(Cr) structure prior to ETEM experiments. Because MOF crystals are known to be beamsensitive materials,28-30 low-dose TEM beam conditions $(\sim5\mathrm{~\e~{~}~}/\mathrm{{\AA}}^{2}$ cumulative dose) were used during in situ ETEM experiments to mitigate potential damage (see Supporting Information for details). Upon insertion into the ETEM column, the MIL-53(Cr) nanocrystals were exposed to ultra-high vacuum, with the substrate at ambient temperature $(27~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . An isolated nanocrystal, generating a strong single-crystalline diffraction pattern, was selected for the subsequent in situ breathing ETEM experiment (Figure 1 and Figures S2, S3, S4).
By comparing the experimental diffraction pattern (Figure 1A and Table 1) to simulated electron diffraction patterns generated by independent theoretical modeling of the MIL-53(Cr) lattice (Figure S5, Table 2), we determined that the selected nanocrystal had the MIL53(Cr)-ht-‘empty’ $h t=$ high-temperature in the original report)13 crystal structure on the [322] zone axis, with a mismatch error of ${\sim}3\%$ (Table S1). Our simulations of the MIL-53(Cr)-‘empty’ structure $(27\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , vacuum) found a lattice with the Imma space group, matching that previously determined by PXRD for MIL-53(Cr)-ht(‘empty’).13 In the as-synthesized form, free terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc)}$ molecules are expected in the pore channels,13 but under the ultra-high vacuum of the microscope column and the initial $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ beam irradiation, the crystal pores were largely emptied of residual $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}$ , solvent, or water molecules (confirmed by additional control TEM experiments in UH-vacuum, Figure S6). This “emptying” transition could not be captured, as it likely occurred upon initial pump-down to UH-vacuum in the ETEM column (prior to the first acquired diffraction pattern in Figure 1a).
Using the ETEM gas manifold system, we introduced $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor into the microscope column at ${\sim}3$ mbar, with directed flow onto the specimen. The spatial resolution was slightly degraded by background scattering caused by the gas molecules in the column, but we note that no changes in either the overall nanoparticle morphology (TEM image) or its crystal structure (diffraction pattern) were observed after ${\sim}25$ min under $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor at $27~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 1b).
Following the previous report,13 the MIL-53(Cr) sample was rapidly heated to, and held at, $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in situ while maintaining ${\sim}3$ mbar $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor within the ETEM. An image and diffraction pattern of the nanoparticle was acquired after ${\sim}15\ \mathrm{min}$ at this condition (Figure 1c). Comparing to Fig. 1b, it is evident that the nanocrystal has experienced a dramatic structural transformation, reflected by the changes in its diffraction pattern, while maintaining overall integrity. A qualitative change in the overall particle dimensions is evident (the width has contracted and the height has extended, Table S2) in the TEM micrographs, with no detectable particle degradation. Despite the significant spot location shifts, the pattern remains single-crystalline, indicating that the nanostructure transition is consistent throughout the entire crystal domain. However, the cause of the shift is unclear from these ETEM data alone: either the lattice nanostructure has changed because of breathing, or possibly the lattice orientation has simply changed, or some combination of the two. Furthermore, without correlating with MD simulations, ETEM diffraction cannot determine the molecular adsorption, or other, processes, that gave rise to the nanostructure transitions.
Seeking insight into the breathing behavior of MIL$53(\mathrm{Cr})$ at the lattice level, MD simulations were independently carried out at $27~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for a variable number of water molecules $(\Nu_{\mathrm{w}})$ adsorbed per unit cell $(\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{w}}=0{-}10$ , 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 23-28, 30, 34, 40, 45, and 50) following a reported procedur e,31,32 which generated a library of modeled lattice structures (see Supporting Information for details). The MD structures were used in STEM_CELL software to simulate electron diffraction patterns (Figure S5).33 As noted above, the [322] zone axis gave the best fit to the experimental ETEM patterns of the selected nanoparticle for an empty MIL-53(Cr) lattice, and this orientation was maintained for the simulated diffraction patterns’ parameters listed in Table 2 for variable amounts of water (unit cell parameters in Table S3).
By comparing the simulated (Table 2) and experimental ETEM (Table 1) diffraction patterns for the 300 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor condition (“calcinated” MIL-53(Cr)- cal), assuming no orientation change ([322] zone axis), we find an excellent agreement $(<1.5\%$ mismatch error, Table S1) for the MIL-53(Cr) lattice containing $1\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule per unit cell (Fig. S5). That is, the nanostructure has undergone a first breathing transition, from Imma to the lower symmetry $P2_{1}$ space group. We note that completely empty pores were predicted for “calcinated” MIL-53(Cr) at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (-ht) in the original report based on temperature-dependent PXRD measurements.13 However, by monitoring a single nanocrystal, we find that for the lattice that has been emptied via lowpressure at room temperature, rather than via ambient pressure heating, the first calcination (heating to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ causes an additional transition (activation), with the lattice acquiring a single $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule. The present analysis thus suggests that correlating in situ experimental lattice data with computer modeling provides unique molecular-level insights into structural transformations induced by temperature changes and guest adsorption in individual MOF nanocrystals.
The ETEM breathing experiment on the same MIL$53(\mathrm{Cr})–c a l$ nanocrystal continued through the next transition (Fig. 1d) by lowering the temperature of the specimen back to $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (from $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) while maintaining $^{\sim3}$ mbar $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor inside the ETEM. The comparison of the ETEM diffraction pattern at this stage (Figure 1d) with those acquired at different conditions (Figures 1a-c) shows that the nanocrystal underwent another significant, but distinctly different, lattice transformation. Upon cooling back to $27\ {^\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (after ${\sim}14\ \mathrm{h}$ ), the diffraction pattern did not return to the initial room temperature state (“empty”), but instead transformed into an entirely new nanostructure (still single-crystalline). To interpret this diffraction transition, the ETEM pattern was matched with various patterns on the [322] zone axis obtained from MD simulations for variable numbers of water molecules adsorbed in the pores. An excellent fit $(<4.5\%$ mismatch) was found for the MIL-53(Cr)- postcal lattice at $27\ {^\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\left(-l t\right)$ containing $25~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules per unit cell (Fig. S5, Table 2, and Table S1), which is characterized by the low symmetry $P1$ space group. This nanostructure assignment is consistent with the ETEM images, where the particle orientation appears unchanged during the transition, but where the particle dimensions have once again changed slightly (now the width has expanded and the height has contracted, Table S2). Additional heating-cooling cycles found reversible transitions between the $\mathrm{1-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(P2_{1})$ and $25\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(P1)$ lattice structures (Fig. S3).
In summary, for the MIL-53(Cr) nanocrystal characterized in this study, we find that breathing induced by water adsorption in the MOF pores proceeds by: 1) the removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}$ from the pore channels when in UHvacuum and $\mathrm{e^{-}}$ beam irradiation, with no change after the introduction of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor, 2) upon heating to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (calcination), $\mathrm{1H_{2}O}$ molecule per unit cell is adsorbed in the pore channels (lattice change), and 3) when the temperature is lowered back to $27~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , an additional $24~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules per unit cell are adsorbed in the pores (further lattice change). This breathing transition between 1 and $25~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules is reversible by heating-cooling cycling. Adsorption of a single water molecule per unit cell during the first calcination (heating to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) represents the initial step that activates the breathing effect, providing a means for including up to $25\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules when subsequently cooled to $27\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As shown by the MD simulations, this activation step involves hydrogen bonding between the first adsorbed water molecules and the bridging $\upmu{-}\mathrm{OH}$ groups of the framework. By anchoring to the framework, these individual $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules become effective hydrogen bonding sites that facilitate the adsorption of additional water molecules when the material is cooled, and they remain when other water molecules are removed from the pores during subsequent heating.
By monitoring the lattice structure of a single MOF nanocrystal in situ and correlating the results with MD simulations, we precisely determined the extent of water adsorption/desorption during the breathing cycle. The combination of ETEM measurements and computer simulations is shown to provide molecular-level insights into water-framework interactions and their effects on overall crystal structures. We propose that the same integrated ETEM-MD approach is directly applicable for the study of other similar environmentally-induced lattice transformations in MOFs or other mesoporous nanomaterials.
